from author_proof_obligations import add
add('P177-0001','induction supplied with edge argument suppressed','Theorem 3.8.1, Corollary 4: acyclic bases','The stated vanishing on finite intersections and local refinements forces the canonical Cech-to-derived map to be an isomorphism.','Induct in the cohomological degree, use the global cover spectral sequence after passing to a cofinal family, and control the q=0 edge and the first possible positive row. The corrected degree n, rather than the stray q, is required in the induction conclusion.','acyclic basis|Cech comparison|spectral sequence')
add('P177-0002','printed exact sequence has a false terminal zero','§3.8: low-degree Cech comparison sequence','The map from H^2 to Cech H^1 of the H^1 presheaf need not be surjective; the next differential lands in Cech H^3.','Retain the next term of the spectral-sequence edge sequence. The finite-space computation recorded in this run gives nonzero next differential and refutes the terminal zero. The special example that follows may still have surjectivity, but it cannot justify the unrestricted truncated sequence.','Cech H3|five-term exact sequence|transgression')
add('P178-0001','special-cover calculation sketched','§3.8: the Cech H2 comparison example','For the specified two-intersection-point geometry, the special covers give the asserted degree-one cocycles and a nonzero cohomology-presheaf class.','Check that those covers are cofinal, compute every relevant intersection and restriction map, and verify that the two surviving ordered pairs have opposite values by the cocycle equation. The two intersection points and the corrected subscripts are essential to this computation.','Cech comparison counterexample|cohomology presheaf|two points')
add('P179-0001','theorem requires corrected closure hypothesis','Theorem 3.9.1: acyclicity from a chosen family of covers','The induction works when traces of every chosen cover on every member of every other chosen cover remain in the assigned family.','Replace the merely self-trace condition by closure under cross-traces. The torus local-system counterexample recorded in this run satisfies the weaker printed condition but violates the conclusion. With the stronger condition the induction on the number of covers becomes legitimate.','acyclicity criterion|cross trace|cover family','TOH-3.9.1-printed|TOH-3.9.1-corrected')
add('P179-0002','corollary depends on repaired theorem','Theorem 3.9.1, Corollary 1','Under the corrected cover-family hypotheses, the vanishing criterion is equivalent to A(n) and B(n).','For sufficiency apply the repaired theorem. For necessity use vanishing on all finite intersections to collapse the fixed-cover spectral sequence through degree n; B(n) is immediate from the assumed vanishing.','acyclicity criterion|cover family|A(n)|B(n)')
add('P179-0003','spectral-sequence edge verification suppressed','Theorem 3.9.1, Corollary 1: necessity','Under low-degree acyclicity of all intersection members, fixed-cover Cech cohomology agrees with derived cohomology through the specified range.','Show all rows q=1 through n vanish already at the cochain level, inspect possible incoming and outgoing differentials in total degree at most n, and use the finite filtration of the first-quadrant abutment. Vanishing of H^0 is not asserted.','Cech spectral sequence|low degree|acyclic intersection')
add('P179-0004','cofinality corollary depends on repaired closure','Theorem 3.9.1, Corollary 2','A cofinal supply of finite intersections of chosen covers turns fixed-cover vanishing into sheaf acyclicity under the corrected trace hypothesis.','Combine local vanishing of positive cohomology classes with the stated cofinality to verify B(n), then apply Corollary 1 with the strengthened cover-family assumption.','cofinal cover|acyclicity|refinement')
add('P179-0005','brief proof with local-to-cover step','Theorem 3.9.1, Corollary 2: verification of B(n)','Local vanishing of a fixed cohomology class can be arranged on a finite intersection of selected covers when that family is cofinal.','First form the open cover on which the class vanishes, then choose the finite set of designated covers whose common refinement refines it. Distinguish finiteness of the set of covers from finiteness of each cover.','local vanishing|cofinal refinement|B(n)')
add('P180-0001','bounded-cover corollary proof compressed','Theorem 3.9.1, Corollary 3','A degree bound for the relevant cover complexes extends the finite-range vanishing criterion to all positive degrees.','Verify the vanishing in degrees above the cover bound using a normalized or alternating complex with its justified comparison, then apply the repaired preceding corollary for every degree.','cover dimension|Cech complex|acyclicity')
add('P180-0002','induction reveals missing printed hypothesis','Theorem 3.9.1: induction on the number of covers','The cross-trace condition is exactly what allows restriction of the remaining covers to a member of the first cover.','Use the spectral-sequence edge injection for one cover. For k covers restrict the class and the other k−1 covers to each member of the first, then invoke the induction only after verifying those traces belong to the required families. The original self-trace hypothesis does not supply this step.','cross trace|induction|cover family','TOH-3.9.1-corrected')
add('P181-0001','application sketched','§3.9: compact cubes and two-piece decompositions','For compact cubes and coordinate-hyperplane decompositions, the two-piece gluing condition gives the stated acyclicity criterion.','Verify finite closed-cover spectral sequences, closure under cross-traces and finite intersections, and cofinality of finite coordinate subdivisions using compactness. The two-set Cech complex reduces the hypothesis to surjectivity of the difference of restrictions on the intersection.','Cartan theorem|compact cube|two-set cover')
add('P181-0002','nonabelian degree-one extension left unproved','§3.9, Remark: nonabelian H1 version','The degree-one torsor version follows by gluing local trivializations under the same corrected cover-family closure.','Replace additive cocycles and their vanishing by transition functions and coboundaries, prove triviality first for one chosen cover, and repeat the restriction induction for finitely many covers. The trace hypothesis remains necessary to that proof.','nonabelian H1|torsor|Cartan gluing')
add('P181-0003','proof continued on following page','Proposition 3.10.1: filtered limits of functors','With exact filtered colimits in the target, deriving commutes with the filtered colimit of the specified functors.','Use one injective resolution of the argument, commute the termwise filtered colimit with cohomology, and identify the resulting natural map and connecting morphisms. The assertion concerns a colimit of functors; it is distinct from commuting a fixed functor with a colimit of objects.','filtered colimit of functors|derived functor')
add('P182-0001','neighborhood hypothesis needs qualification','Proposition 3.10.1, Corollary 2: neighborhood cohomology','The neighborhood-limit comparison uses a fundamental system of neighborhoods satisfying the stated paracompact extension conditions.','Prove the degree-zero extension of sections to a neighborhood and effaceability of the limiting positive-degree functors using suitable acyclic resolutions. Local compactness plus paracompactness of X does not automatically give the parenthetical assertion for every subset; the ordinal-space counterexample recorded in this run shows the needed closedness/neighborhoood qualification.','neighborhood cohomology|paracompact|closed subset')
add('P182-0002','inverse-limit result introduced without proof','§3.10, equation (3.10.2): cohomology on an exhaustion','Restriction gives a natural map from global cohomology to the inverse limit over relatively compact open subsets, and it is an isomorphism in degree zero.','Use the sheaf gluing axiom to identify compatible degree-zero sections. For higher degrees choose a countable cofinal exhaustion when available and account for the derived inverse-limit obstruction; ordinary inverse limits are not exact in general.','inverse limit|exhaustion|lim1')
add('P183-0001','proposition explicitly unproved','Proposition 3.10.2: countable exhaustion and Mittag–Leffler','For a countable exhaustion the comparison is surjective; stabilization of the images in the preceding degree gives injectivity.','Choose a nested relatively compact cofinal exhaustion, derive the Milnor short exact sequence with lim^1 H^(p−1), and prove lim^1 vanishes under the stated Mittag–Leffler image condition. The complete statement was read from the corrected p183 source because the cue extractor bounded this fragment mid-formula.','Mittag Leffler|lim1|countable exhaustion')
add('P183-0002','topological approximation and nonabelian extension unproved','Proposition 3.10.2: complete metrizable approximation','In degree one, the stated density condition on continuous maps between complete metrizable section groups yields injectivity; the neutral-fibre assertion extends to nonabelian groups.','Give the successive-approximation construction with summably shrinking neighborhoods, use completeness to obtain compatible corrected sections, and prove the corrections kill the lim^1 obstruction. For nonabelian groups use the corresponding pointed orbit set and ordered products. The whole p183 continuation, including density and the final remark, was read separately.','Mittag Leffler approximation|complete metrizable group|nonabelian lim1')
add('P185-0001','sheaf and scalar-action verification omitted','§4.1: global and internal Hom','Local Hom forms a sheaf, and global Hom is its group of sections; the internal Hom carries the action of the sheaf center.','Glue local morphisms through the sheaf axioms and check module linearity locally. Define the sheaf center by commutation with all local sections after restriction; the center of the ring of global sections need not restrict into it. That distinction is recorded separately in the counterexample notes.','internal Hom|sheaf center|local morphism')
add('P185-0002','comparison map and finiteness definitions','Proposition 4.1.1 setup: stalk of internal Hom','There is a natural map from the stalk of internal Hom to Hom of stalks; finite generation yields injectivity and finite presentation yields bijectivity.','For injectivity shrink a common neighborhood for finitely many generators. For surjectivity lift the finitely many generator images and shrink again to impose finitely many relations. Do not identify the two Hom groups without these hypotheses. The historical pseudo-coherent terminology here means the displayed finite presentation.','stalk Hom|finite presentation|pseudo-coherent')
add('P186-0001','reduction to a local finite-presentation condition','§4.1: when an injective sheaf has injective stalks','The Baer criterion reduces stalk injectivity to representing each ideal and its map by a local finitely presented submodule.','Find the neighborhood ideal with the prescribed stalk, use Proposition 4.1.1 to realize the map locally, and extend it using injectivity of the open restriction. Without the stated local finiteness hypotheses, injectivity of a sheaf does not imply injectivity of every stalk.','injective stalk|Baer criterion|coherent ring sheaf')
add('P187-0001','restriction argument needs open subsets','§4.2: local Ext and restriction maps','For open U and V, derived Hom on the original module category computes Ext on U and induces the indicated restriction maps.','Use exact open restriction and its preservation of injectives. Ext on an arbitrary subspace can be defined separately, but the claimed identification with the derived functor on X requires the open-set hypothesis or a replacement acyclicity argument. The recorded Sierpinski example disproves the unrestricted inference.','local Ext|open restriction|injective restriction')
